course: religion-ethics
lesson: 22
title: What Christians believe: prayer, forgiveness, grace and heaven
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Christians believe prayer is talking and listening to God, alone or together: saying thank you, saying sorry, asking for others and asking for ourselves.
explain: They believe God hears every prayer, but the answer may be yes, no or wait, the way a loving parent does not hand over everything a child asks for; prayer is a friendship, not a vending machine.
explain: Sin means turning away from God and doing wrong in thought, word or deed; Christians say everyone does it sometimes, and that God forgives anyone who is truly sorry because of Jesus.
explain: Grace is God's love given as a free gift, not as wages, and faith is trusting God the way you trust a parent, not just knowing facts about him; forgiven people are asked to forgive others too, even «seventy times seven» times.
explain: Christians hope for eternal life with God, which they say begins now and goes on after death because Jesus rose again; heaven is pictured as a place with no more pain or tears.
explain: For a child today this means you can start again after doing wrong, and you can give others a fresh start too, without pretending the wrong did not matter.
fact: Christians believe prayer is talking and listening to God, alone or with others.
fact: Prayer can include thanks, saying sorry, asking for others and asking for ourselves.
fact: Christians believe God hears every prayer, but the answer may be yes, no or wait.
fact: Why some prayers seem unanswered is a hard question that Christians still discuss.
fact: Sin is turning away from God and doing wrong in thought, word or deed.
fact: Christians believe everyone sins sometimes, so everyone needs forgiveness.
fact: Christians believe God forgives anyone who is truly sorry, because of Jesus.
fact: Jesus told Peter to forgive not seven times but «seventy times seven» times, meaning without keeping count.
fact: Forgiving does not mean pretending nothing happened or that the wrong does not matter.
fact: Grace is God's love and help given freely; Christians believe it cannot be earned.
fact: Faith means trusting God, not only believing facts about him.
fact: Christians believe heaven is being with God for ever, with no more pain or tears.
fact: Christians say what heaven is like is beyond description; wings, harps and clouds are only pictures from paintings and cartoons.
fact: Many Christians believe God will judge everyone fairly and with mercy; beliefs about hell differ widely and are disputed.
fact: Christians believe eternal life starts now and continues after death because Jesus rose again.
exercise: What is prayer, as Christians understand it? | Talking and listening to God | Reciting magic words to get things | Talking only to dead relatives | Listening to the vicar's sermon
exercise: Which of these is a kind of prayer? | Saying thank you to God for a good day | Shouting at a friend who cheated | Reading a comic quietly in the church hall | Counting the coins in the collection
exercise: Christians say God answers prayer in three ways. Which three? | Yes, no or wait | Yes, yes or yes | Now, tomorrow or never | Loud, quiet or silent
exercise: Why do Christians say prayer is not a vending machine? | It is a relationship with God, not a deal | Vending machines are not allowed in church | God only answers prayers on Sundays | You have to pay before God listens
exercise: What is sin? | Turning away from God and doing wrong | Making a mistake in a sum at school today | Feeling sad when someone has been unkind | Missing church on Sunday because you are ill
exercise: Can sin be in thoughts as well as actions? | Yes, in thought, word or deed | No, only actions can be sins | No, only words spoken aloud | Yes, but only thoughts at night
exercise: Why do Christians say everyone needs forgiveness? | Because everyone sins sometimes | Because God likes people to feel bad | Because only bad people go to church | Because babies are born criminals
exercise: Who does God forgive, according to Christians? | Anyone who is truly sorry | Only people who never sin again | Only people who give money to church | Only priests and nuns
exercise: What did Jesus mean by forgiving «seventy times seven» times? | Keep forgiving without counting | Stop forgiving after 490 times | Forgive exactly seven people a day | Only forgive people who say sorry twice
exercise: Is forgiving the same as pretending nothing happened? | No, the wrong still matters, but you let go of revenge | Yes, you must forget it ever happened | Yes, and you must let it happen again | No, forgiving means getting even first
exercise: What is grace? | God's love given freely, not earned | A reward for being good all week long | A prayer said only before Sunday dinner | A prize for learning lots of Bible verses
exercise: How is faith different from just knowing facts about God? | Faith is trusting God like a parent | Faith is knowing more facts than others | Faith is going to church every week | Faith is never having any questions
exercise: What do Christians believe heaven is? | Being with God for ever, with no more pain | A city in the clouds where people turn into angels | A reward shop for good behaviour points | A place only for people who died old
exercise: What do many Christians believe about judgement? | God will judge everyone fairly and with mercy | God will judge only people of other faiths | Judgement is done by the Pope each year | Nobody's actions matter to God at all
exercise: When does eternal life begin, according to Christians? | Now, and it continues after death | Only on the day the world ends | At the age of eighteen | At baptism, and it stops at death
exercise: Why do Christians link eternal life with Easter? | Because Jesus rose from death | Because eggs are a sign of long life | Because spring lasts for ever | Because Easter Sunday is the longest day
test: «prayed for a new bike and got nothing, so prayer's fake innit» says your mate. What would a Christian say? | God hears, but the answer may be no or wait | God only listens to adults | He didn't pray loud enough | Bikes are sinful so God got angry
test: Your sister snapped at you, then said sorry. Next day she did it again. Your nan says «forgive her». What does Jesus' teaching mean here? | Keep forgiving, while still telling her it hurts | Forgive only once, then never again, whatever she says | Forgive her only if she pays you back | Pretend it never happened and say nothing
test: Leo, who isn't religious, says: «So you can do anything, say sorry and God's fine with it?» Best reply? | Forgiveness needs real sorrow and a wish to change | Yes, just saying the word sorry is all it ever takes | No, God never forgives anything serious | Only if you also pay the church a fine
test: «I got God's love cos I'm the best in Sunday school,» brags a boy. What's the Christian answer? | God's love is a free gift, not a prize | Yes, the best pupil gets the most love | He'll lose God's love if he comes second | God's love is earned by memorising verses
test: An olympiad judge asks: «How can trusting a person be different from knowing about them?» Best answer? | You can know facts about someone you do not rely on | Trusting someone just means that you know their birthday | There is no difference at all | Knowing is for adults, trusting is for kids
test: «My grandad's in heaven now, he's got wings and a harp,» says your cousin. What do Christians actually say? | He is with God; what heaven is like is beyond description | Yes, everyone becomes an angel with wings | No, Christians do not believe in heaven | He is waiting in a cloud for his birthday
test: «You Christians think everyone else burns in hell, right?» says a classmate angrily. Fairest reply? | Beliefs about hell differ widely; many trust God to be fair and merciful | Yes, all Christians believe exactly that | No Christian has ever believed in hell | Only people from other countries go there
test: Your friend says, «If I've done something bad, I'm just a bad person, full stop.» What does the Christian idea of forgiveness say? | You can be forgiven and start again | Once bad, always bad, nothing can change it | Only good luck can make you good again | Bad people should stop going to church
test: «why'd you pray for my mum when she's not even christian?» asks a friend. What's the best answer? | Christians pray for everyone they care about | Prayers only work for people who are Christians | I was secretly trying to make her join my church | Praying for other people is against the rules
test: A judge follows up: «Is forgiving someone the same as forgetting?» | No, you can remember and still let go of revenge | Yes, real forgiving wipes your memory | Yes, if you remember, you have not forgiven | No, you must punish them first, and only then forget it happened
test: «the line in the prayer says forgive us as we forgive, but I'm still mad at Jake lol» — what is the honest step? | Ask God for help to forgive Jake | Stop saying that prayer for ever | Pretend you forgave him and move on | Get Jake back first, then forgive
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the Christian conception of grace? | The love and assistance of God bestowed freely and unmerited | A recompense accorded in proportion to good conduct | A formal blessing pronounced exclusively at mealtimes | An entitlement acquired through regular attendance
exam: Identify the response consistent with the Christian understanding of petitionary prayer. | That God attends to every prayer, the answer being yes, no or wait | That God grants without exception every request made | That prayer is efficacious only when uttered by clergy | That God attends solely to prayers offered on Sundays in a church building
exam: Ascertain the meaning of the injunction to forgive «seventy times seven» times. | That forgiveness ought to be extended without reckoning | That forgiveness ceases after four hundred and ninety offences | That seven persons only are to be forgiven daily | That forgiveness is contingent upon a sevenfold apology
exam: Select the statement most consistent with Christian teaching concerning eternal life. | That it commences in the present and endures beyond death | That it is conferred solely upon the attainment of old age | That it terminates upon the death of the believer | That it is reserved exclusively for members of the clergy
